Given, numbers : $31,32,33, \ldots, 46,47$. The standard deviation of $31,32,33, \ldots, 47$ will be the same as those of $1,2,3, \ldots, 17$. (decreasing each item by 30 )
$\because \quad \mathrm{SD}=\sqrt{\frac{n^{2}-1}{12}}$
Here, $\quad n=17\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad \mathrm{SD} &=\sqrt{\frac{17^{2}-1}{12}}=\sqrt{\frac{288}{12}} \\
&=\sqrt{24}=2 \sqrt{6}
\end{aligned}$

As we know that, the volume of sphere is given by<br>\[
V=\frac{4}{3} \Pi r^{3}
\]<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
\text { So, } \frac{d V}{d t}=4 \Pi r^{2} \frac{d r}{d t} \\
\text { Here } \frac{d V}{d t}=10 \mathrm{~cm}^{3} \mathrm{~s}^{-1} \text { and } r=15 \mathrm{~cm} \text { we get } \\
10=4 \Pi(15)^{2} \frac{d r}{d t} \\
\Rightarrow \frac{d r}{d t}=\frac{10}{4 \Pi\left(15^{2}\right)}=\frac{1}{90 \Pi}
\end{array}
\]
